From 46a6e4f18a1b19dd97dc44b6ae866c3c68b008e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: i <v@g>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 18:16:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.supplier: sign the advisory, because the forged one
 reflashes a robot
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A component advisory reaches an integrator as a PDF attached to an email.
Nothing about it is verifiable. So this works:

    From:    security@controlco-support.example
    Subject: URGENT — safety controller firmware 3.9.0 advisory
    Body:    Action required within 24 hours. Flash the attached image.

An integrator who acts on that has been induced to modify the safety system of
a machine people stand next to. That is not a data breach with a notification
attached; it is a physical safety incident delivered by email. The industry's
present defence is that the recipient recognises the sender's writing style.

This closes it, and the reason nobody has is not that it is hard.

  assurance supplier keygen     an Ed25519 publishing key, and the fingerprint
                                to hand customers by a route advisories do not
                                travel on
  assurance supplier publish    sign an advisory into your feed
  assurance supplier withdraw   sign a withdrawal. The original is never deleted
  assurance supplier verify     check a feed before anything acts on it

The feed is a chain, not a list, and three properties follow.

**A supplier cannot silently un-publish.** Each record names the content hash of
the previous one. The commercial incentive on a supplier who has published an
embarrassing advisory is to make it go away; removing a record now breaks the
chain at a named position in every copy every customer already holds.

**A withdrawal is a publication.** A supplier who got one wrong publishes a
signed withdrawal naming it, with a reason. The original stays: integrators
already changed machines because of it, and erasing it leaves them holding a
modification to a safety system that nobody can explain.

**An empty feed does not verify.** A wrong path and a supplier with nothing to
report look identical in the file, and "your fleet is unaffected" is the worst
possible wrong answer to give somebody who is exposed. Both are refused rather
than guessed between.

One feed, one supplier, because an integrator pins one key to one feed and two
suppliers sharing a file makes that pinning meaningless. An advisory whose
issued_by is not the publisher is refused, because a feed carrying somebody
else's advisory under your signature makes you the source of it.

The refusal that matters is in `assurance fleet advisory`. Given --feed it
verifies before it computes, and a feed that does not hold up is refused with
exit 2 and nothing calculated — not a warning, a refusal. --unsigned-anyway
exists, prints the full reason, and records it in the output under provenance,
because sometimes you have to proceed and everyone downstream should know you
did. An unsigned advisory file passed the old way still gets the warning: a
tool that treats signed and unsigned as equivalent has closed nothing.

`publish` says so every time an advisory names versions but no content hashes,
because that is the difference between telling a customer "possible — somebody
has to go and look at the machine" and "affected, by hash, beyond argument" —
and it is the single most useful thing a component supplier can do for the
people who install their parts.

What a verified feed does not establish is in checks_skipped on every verdict:
that the key belongs to the supplier, which cryptography cannot bootstrap and
which is why the fingerprint is printed everywhere; and that the advisories are
correct, complete or timely, because a supplier who has not noticed a
vulnerability publishes nothing and no amount of signing detects silence.

Commercially this is the second payer and the reason the first one stays. A
supplier publishes once; every integrator with that component enrolled learns
which of their serial numbers it touches and which safety functions it puts in
question. That fan-out only works for people whose machines are enrolled here.

Tests: 30 new, 701 total, all passing. ruff clean, mypy clean on the new module.
The load-bearing one alters the remedy text of a published advisory to point at
an attacker's firmware image and asserts that both `supplier verify` and
`fleet advisory` refuse it.

Verified end to end before committing: a supplier key generated, a real advisory
signed into a feed, an integrator's fleet of two machines enrolled offline by
the kit, the signed feed fanned across it — two machines matched, reported as
possible rather than affected because ControlCo published no hashes, and the
report saying exactly that — then the same feed with one word changed, refused.

